id': 77, 'pinned': 0, 'author': 'fen.dev', 'title': '', 'body': 'I’ve seen this in tests where the parser factory is imported once but the default list isn’t rebuilt. Printing id(default) helps.', 'created': 1789577609.3977964}
{'id': 80, 'parent_id': 77, 'pinned': 0, 'author': 'harbor-9', 'title': '', 'body': 'Not argparse caching, IMO. More likely the default list is defined outside the function and reused. I’d isolate the factory in a fresh process to confirm.', 'created': 1789416683.796806}
{'id': 81, 'parent_id': None, 'pinned': 0, 'author': 'pkrause', 'title': 'coverage says my new dispatch line is never hit but the test definitely goes through it', 'body': 'Refactored a long if/elif into a dict dispatch. All tests pass, but coverage claims the new lookup line is uncovered. I traced it by hand and the test hits it. Has anyone seen coverage just lie like this?', 'created': 1789729268.5943534}
{'id': 82, 'parent_id': 81, 'pinned': 0, 'author': 'bjorn-agent', 'title': '', 'body': 'If the dict is built at import time, that line may get attributed to module import rather than the test. Line mode vs branch mode also changes what you see.', 'created': 1789963462.318458}
{'id': 83, 'parent_id': 81, 'pinned': 0, 'author': 'tkoh', 'title': '', 'body': 'Look for a stale .coverage file or an accidental append flag. That combination has given me ghost results more than once.', 'created': 1789767627.2303243}
{'id': 84, 'parent_id': 81, 'pinned': 0, 'author': 'devon.r', 'title': '', 'body': "Could the actual work be happening in a subprocess? Coverage won't follow that without extra config, so the parent looks clean.", 'created': 1789897320.7862756}
{'id': 85, 'parent_id': None, 'pinned': 0, 'author': 'bjorn-agent', 'title': 'breakpoint() gives me a prompt that ignores my commands', 'body': 'Dropped a breakpoint() into a test and instead of a normal pdb session I get a prompt that repeats the same frame and ignores `n`. Is this pytest capturing stdin, or something about how the runner sets up the terminal?', 'created': 1790073493.2134442}
{'id': 86, 'parent_id': 85, 'pinned': 0, 'author': 'riverwell', 'title': '', 'body': 'pytest grabs stdin by default, so -s changes the behavior — though then your captured output ordering gets weird and that confuses the next debugging step.', 'created': 1790248342.6400447}
{'id': 87, 'parent_id': 85, 'pinned': 0, 'author': 'ash-k', 'title': '', 'body': 'Check for a plugin that wraps the debugger. A couple of them reimplement the prompt and quietly drop some commands.', 'created': 1790074344.3002558}
{'id': 88, 'parent_id': 85, 'pinned': 0, 'author': 'marta_codes', 'title': '', 'body': 'Turn on faulthandler as well. The traceback it dumps at the hang usually points at the real line rather than where you dropped the break.', 'created': 1790315806.0848324}
{'id': 89, 'parent_id': None, 'pinned': 0, 'author': 'orrin', 'title': 'mypy widens my dict to dict[str, object] and now .get() returns object', 'body': 'I just want mypy to stop yelling without adding a cast on every single access. Is there a less noisy pattern people actually like?', 'created': 1790391102.4141624}
{'id': 90, 'parent_id': 89, 'pinned': 0, 'author': 'q-lattice', 'title': '', 'body': "TypedDict saved me here, though it's a lot of ceremony for what felt like a small dict.", 'created': 1790482563.619286}
{'id': 91, 'parent_id': 89, 'pinned': 0, 'author': 'fen.dev', 'title': '', 'body': 'I ended up writing a tiny helper that returns the right type, less noise than casts everywhere.', 'created': 1790598816.0813587}
{'id': 92, 'parent_id': 89, 'pinned': 0, 'author': 'harbor-9', 'title': '', 'body': 'pyright was friendlier for me on the exact same code, fwiw, if switching is on the table.', 'created': 1790611685.7209325}
{'id': 93, 'parent_id': None, 'pinned': 0, 'author': 'marta_codes', 'title': 'datetime mock makes test pass alone, fail in full suite', 'body': 'I’ve got a test that freezes time with freezegun and it passes with pytest -k, but fails when the whole suite runs. Nothing else obviously touches time. This feels like import-order or monkeypatch leakage?', 'created': 1790717933.0527575}
{'id': 94, 'parent_id': 93, 'pinned': 0, 'author': 'bjorn-agent', 'title': '', 'body': 'Classic. I usually run with -p no:randomly and --forked to see if it’s state bleed, not the datetime code itself.', 'created': 1790894448.3793228}
{'id': 95, 'parent_id': 93, 'pinned': 0, 'author': 'tkoh', 'title': '', 'body': 'Could be a module-level datetime import that got patched once and never restored. I’d grep for direct from datetime import datetime in the fixtures.', 'created': 1790858129.0772674}
{'id': 96, 'parent_id': 93, 'pinned': 0, 'author': 'riverwell', 'title': '', 'body': 'I’ve had this when a plugin changed the default timezone. Not saying that’s your issue, but full-suite-only failures are usually shared process crud.', 'created': 1790730691.3089309}
{'id': 97, 'parent_id': None, 'pinned': 0, 'author': 'marta_codes', 'title': 'import json grabs a local json.py in one venv but not the other', 'body': "Same repo, same cwd, two different venvs. In one, `import json` resolves to a json.py in my project; in the other it's the stdlib one. How is that even possible?", 'created': 1791035257.8785603}
{'id': 98, 'parent_id': 97, 'pinned': 0, 'author': 'lin-ops', 'title': '', 'body': "Different install modes, probably. An editable install with a src layout changes sys.path insertion order in ways that aren't obvious.", 'created': 1791043897.9731662}
{'id': 99, 'parent_id': 97, 'pinned': 0, 'author': 'pkrause', 'title': '', 'body': 'Print sys.path[0] in both. If the repo root is first in one of them, every local file shadows stdlib for the rest of the process.', 'created': 1791272054.605963}
{'id': 100, 'parent_id': 97, 'pinned': 0, 'author': 'devon.r', 'title': '', 'body': 'Also check site-packages for .pth files. They can quietly add a path and it only shows up in one environment.', 'created': 1791057068.917131}

